{#- One self-contained block per role: title, employer, website, then the body.

   classic used to nest an employer's roles under a single company header. With
   the title on top there is nowhere left for such a header to go -- a header
   can only precede what it heads -- so classic now flattens through
   flatten_roles exactly as ats and txt already did, and a promotion prints as
   two blocks that repeat the employer. Bruno chose that over keeping the group
   and leaving the second role a bare title line: every block then states the
   same three things in the same order, and no entry is a special case of
   another.

   What that gives up is the employer's combined tenure -- BIS's "(3 years)"
   across both roles -- which no longer has a line of its own to sit on. Each
   block states its own dates and duration and the reader adds them up, which
   is what ats and txt have always asked of them. #}
{% for item in experience | flatten_roles %}
	{#- Title bold, employer italic, dates right-aligned on the title line:
	   they date the title, and the employer line below reads the same
	   whichever of its roles it sits under. The weight difference is what
	   separates the two lines; the title used to be indented for that job,
	   which left it the one line in the entry with a different left edge. #}
	{\bf {{ item.title | latex_escape }}} \hfill {\sl {{ item.duration.start | as_date(fmt_month_year) }} -- {{ item.duration.end | as_date(fmt_month_year) }} ({{ parse_duration(item.duration.start, item.duration.end) }})} \\
	{#- \cvsubitemskip tightens the last header line into the body, so it rides
	   on the website line when there is one and falls back to this one when
	   there is not. #}
	{\sl {{ item.company.name | latex_escape }}{% if item.location %}, {{ item.location | latex_escape }}{% endif %}} \\{% if not item.company.url %}[\cvsubitemskip]{% endif %}
	{%- if item.company.url %}
	{\sl — {% if links %}\href{ {{ item.company.url }} }{ {{ item.company.url }} }{% else %}\weburl{ {{- item.company.url -}} }{% endif %}} \\[\cvsubitemskip]
	{%- endif %}
	{#- A household name (Google, a national central bank, ...) doesn't need a
	   blurb explaining what it is; that space is better spent on the role
	   itself. company.show_description is resolved once in generate_cv.py
	   from companies[].is_well_known and the build-wide --company-descriptions
	   level (min/mid/max) -- read the one flag here rather than either input
	   separately.

	   Flattening prints it once per role rather than once per employer, which
	   is what ats and txt have always done. No grouped employer is blurbed at
	   any level today (BIS, the only one, is is_well_known), so this repeats
	   in the sample fixture at --company-descriptions max and nowhere else. #}
	{%- if item.company.description and item.company.show_description %}
	\\
	{{ item.company.description | latex_escape }}
	{%- endif %}
	{%- if item.description %}
	\\
	{{ item.description | latex_escape }}
	{%- endif %}
	{%- if item.achievements %}
	\begin{itemize}
		{%- for achievement in item.achievements %}
		\item {{ achievement | latex_escape }}
		{%- endfor %}
	\end{itemize}
	{%- else %}
	\\
	\newline
	{%- endif %}
	{\sl Technologies: {{ item.technologies | join(', ') | latex_escape }} }
	\\
{% endfor %}
